    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d252-a1">

                <pb facs="0308" n="240" sameAs="frus:frus1867p1/pg240"/>

                <head ana="#head_untitled-missing-head-in-printed-volume">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#center">[From the Moniteur, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1867-04-30">April 30, 1867</date>.—Translation.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>At the time when late events relative to the Duchy of Luxembourg had given rise
                    to apprehensions for the maintenance of peace, the French army in consequence of
                    reductions effected in 1865 had fallen below its established effective strength.
                    In addition the corps of occupation of Mexico in returning to France had left in
                    America 7,000 horses, of which 3,000 were draught horses, which it was
                    indispensable to replace.</p>
                <p>It was therefore the duty of the government to take measures of precaution. These
                    have consisted in raising the strength of the regiment, in buying a certain
                    number of horses, and in putting the frontier forts in a state of defence.</p>
                <p>The pacific news of the last few days have determined the Emperor to order that
                    no new measures be taken, with a view to give public opinion no pretext for
                    excitement nor to diminish the hopes of peace.</p>
                <p>Thus the number of horses to be bought will be limited to what is absolutely
                    necessary, and soldiers on leave, and who were to have been recalled, will be
                    permitted to remain at home.</p>
            </div>
